{"t": 269.225, "n": 129, "ev": "result", "ok": true, "text": "I wrote the plan to `plugins/temperspec-tgraph/docs/plans/i173.plan.md`, with status `planned` and complexity `complex`. I haven't run any check on it, and the gate decides whether it is correct.\n\n- **Why `complex`:** the change touches far more than 10 files and its reading set is far over 200 KB (`outcome.json` alone is about 2,000 lines). The plan holds phases, goals and weights only. Phase 2 is marked `heavy (large-context)`, because a rowless complex plan must carry one heavy phase (`tests/test_code_mode.py` lines 610-621).\n- **Section 3:** the plan lists three verification commands. The proof table is left for the expand step, as the plan rule says for a complex plan.\n- **Requirements:** R1 to R13 name every acceptance criterion, AC 1 to AC 10. Each follows from IRV, RVF or PRD behaviour ids. R11 exists because PRD-AC1 to PRD-AC8 are new criteria, so the existing description tests must carry those ids. IRV-AC1 to IRV-AC14 and RVF-AC1 to RVF-AC7 are also new, so the new tests must carry them as well.\n- **Design choices** (full detail in section 0):\n  - There is one new unit, `independent_review`, so the package test that wants a spec file per unit still passes.\n  - `code_join` becomes a route. A quick-lane run goes to `finalizer`, where the outcome gate runs first; any other run goes to the review.\n  - The reader gets the diff and the numbered criteria as files in `.tempergraph/review/`, which are never committed. It writes a draft there, and an `after` script builds the committed `<task id>.review.md`, including the `Earlier review` section.\n  - The gate has a model-free check plus a judge. The check picks the back step by file (IRV-B9 and IRV-B10) and reads the first trip's step from commit subjects. The judge names that same step on a second review, which spends the engine's per-target budget and pauses the run.\n- **Docs to update:**\n  - `README.md`, `diagram/workflow.md`, `diagram/model.json`, `blueprint.json`, `outcome.json`, `authoring.json` and `stamp.json` are listed.\n  - `tempergraph author verify` is not a declared command, so the code step runs it by hand and reports the result.\n- **Not done:** I did not read the whole of `outcome.json` (the second half, past line 1,325) or the plugin's `authoring.json` and `stamp.json`. The expand step's rows for those files should read them first.\n- **Extra files:** I opened some files beyond the hand-off's reading list. Section 0 names each one and says why.\n\nARTIFACT plan: plugins/temperspec-tgraph/docs/plans/i173.plan.md\nARTIFACT complexity: complex", "subtype": "success", "is_error": false, "session_id": "288ec6eb-0c3a-426f-93d4-9936b974dbf2"}
